# Codex as a kiln harness in a Shards microVM (kiln plan 01a1cd8e862e7169a074db3d5fa33f3d, step 20).
# kiln runs Codex's app-server (Kind "codex", crates/kiln/src/harness/kinds.rs), which it speaks
# JSON-RPC to. pin.json pins OpenAI's whole Codex package for each platform, the archive OpenAI's own
# Linux installer installs (pin.sh checks it three ways), into ./codex-<arch>/: harness.json,
# codex-package.json, bin/ (codex, its code-mode host and exec server), codex-resources/ (bwrap) and
# codex-path/ (rg), the layout Codex finds its helpers through. Nothing else installs it, and no sign-in
# is in the image: a person's lives in their HOME volume, in its CODEX_HOME, until step 33 brings sign-ins from
# outside the microVM.

# Every program kiln has Codex run is static, so any base runs it with nothing added and there is no
# libc variant: this default (Debian trixie's buildpack-deps, pinned by digest as step 19 chose it) or a
# musl base. On linux/arm64 the package's own rg is ripgrep's glibc build, so the pin lays ripgrep's
# static musl build of the same version in its place. The package's voice resources are glibc builds,
# but kiln never starts them: its Codex adapter refuses /voice.
ARG BASE=buildpack-deps:trixie-scm@sha256:4f34e1d673fdb65371de9f88bdd32c4e30152fe692b35604aef5c0b3c0e6da10
FROM ${BASE}

# BuildKit sets TARGETARCH in the global scope, so the stage declares it to name each platform's
# harness directory.
ARG TARGETARCH
HARNESS codex FROM ./codex-${TARGETARCH}

# Its network, every part named (default deny; Shards D236, destinations by name): a network for this
# harness alone, which it joins with no flow to any member, and TCP out to the hosts below alone, by
# name, through the network's boundary (--egress) and the microVM's (EXPOSE ... AS egress). Each name
# resolves for it through the agents' resolver, and no other does. Nothing comes in; no other host,
# port or protocol goes out (kiln plan 01a1cd8e862e7169a074db3d5fa33f3d, step 18:
# docs/research/s18-host-in-vm says where each host comes from).
# - api.openai.com: the Responses API, signed in with an API key; chatgpt.com: Codex's Responses
#   through ChatGPT's backend, signed in with ChatGPT; auth.openai.com: that sign-in, its token
#   refresh and its revocation (codex-rs rust-v0.167.2: cli/src/doctor.rs's reachability plan,
#   login/src/server.rs's issuer, login/src/auth/manager.rs's token URLs).
# - host.docker.internal:7070: kiln's MCP at /api/mcp on the machine itself, at kiln's own port (its
#   http_addr's default, 0.0.0.0:7070). A kiln on another port isn't reached: the image doesn't
#   grant it, and kiln says so as it starts the harness.
NETWORK --egress=api.openai.com:443 --egress=auth.openai.com:443 --egress=chatgpt.com:443 --egress=host.docker.internal:7070 provider FOR codex
EXPOSE --harnesses=codex 443/tcp 7070/tcp AS egress FOR provider
CONNECT codex WITH codex ON provider

# Its project is plugged in as it starts, at the path it has on the machine (Shards D235), so kiln
# reads its diffs, HEAD and checkpoints at that same path, and no volume holds it. Its HOME is the
# person's volume, whole: harness.json sets HOME=/home/codex, kiln's one rule for every harness it
# ships (step 18). A harness sees its image read-only, with its own /tmp scratch, so a HOME that is
# writable and kept from run to run is a volume, and what the programs it runs keep in a home (a
# .gitconfig, a tool's sign-in) is kept with it. Codex keeps its config.toml, its sign-in and its
# sessions in its CODEX_HOME beneath it.
# harness.json keeps caches that follow XDG's rule on the scratch (XDG_CACHE_HOME=/tmp/.cache),
# where they were before its HOME moved onto the volume.
# HOME in run.env needs a shards at or after 997f4e47e (v0.8.0 and later), where run.env replaces
# init's HOME=/tmp. The volume is the harness's alone, and outside /tmp, which the scratch covers.
VOLUME --target-kind=harness /home/codex FOR codex
